<h2>The background</h2>
<p>In the husband&rsquo;s divorce petition before the Family Court (West), Tis Hazari, the wife was proceeded ex parte in September 2024. The record was not thin: process-server reports recorded her refusing summons, with affixation at the gate; another attempt recorded her mother&rsquo;s refusal; and service had also been attempted through the District Judge, Ghaziabad, where she resided. Only in December 2025 — after the husband&rsquo;s evidence had been recorded — did she apply under Order IX to be relieved of the ex parte order. The Family Court rejected the application in October 2026, and she invoked Article 227, contending that service through the mother and by affixation was invalid.</p>

<h2>The service question</h2>
<p>Order V of the CPC anticipates exactly this pattern. Rule 15 permits service on an adult member of the family where the defendant cannot be found; Rule 17 directs the serving officer, where the defendant or such person refuses to sign the acknowledgment or cannot be found, to affix a copy of the summons on the outer door and return the original with a report of the circumstances. The Family Court had examined those reports along with the order-sheets, and the High Court found the service valid under Rules 15 and 17. Refusal, in short, is not a shield; the law treats the litigant who turns the process server away as served.</p>

<h2>Article 227 is supervision, not a second appeal</h2>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;this Court is unable to discern any infirmity in the approach adopted by the learned Family Court&rdquo; &mdash; and, on delay, &ldquo;Dilatory tactics are not to be encouraged as held by Hon&rsquo;ble Supreme Court.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>The supervisory jurisdiction reaches jurisdictional error, patent illegality and perversity. It does not re-appreciate the process-server&rsquo;s reports or re-weigh the sufficiency of the petitioner&rsquo;s explanation — those assessments belonged to the Family Court, which had made them on the record. With <em>Bhuwan Mohan Singh v. Meena</em> supplying the background norm of expedition in matrimonial proceedings, the fifteen-month gap between the ex parte order and the recall application, spanning the recording of evidence, was fatal.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For respondents in matrimonial cases.</strong> Refusing summons buys nothing and costs everything: the case proceeds, evidence closes, and the recall threshold rises with every date. If the petition is bad, appear and defeat it; absence concedes the record.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For petitioners proving service.</strong> The process-server&rsquo;s contemporaneous reports are the asset. Where refusal and affixation are properly recorded — and alternate modes attempted — the service foundation will survive challenge years later.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For counsel choosing the remedy.</strong> An Article 227 petition against a reasoned Order IX refusal must identify perversity, not re-argue sufficiency of cause. Where the Family Court has engaged with the record, the supervisory court will almost always stay its hand.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Ex parte matrimonial decrees and orders generate a steady stream of recall and supervisory litigation in Delhi, usually fought over the same two questions this case presents: was the service real, and was the delay explained. The decision aligns with the settled trend — service rules are applied as written, including their refusal-and-affixation machinery, and Article 227 is kept to its constitutional width. The practical moral for litigants is older than the CPC itself: the courtroom consequences of ignoring a summons are always worse than the summons.</p>
